# ==============================================================================
# bat/config.darwin — bat on macOS (a cat clone with syntax highlighting)
# ==============================================================================
#
# WHICH FILE IS THIS
#   This is the **macOS** variant. bat's config format has no conditionals
#   and one setting genuinely differs per platform (see the Theme block), so the
#   package ships `config.darwin` and `config.linux` and install.sh symlinks
#   ~/.config/bat/config at the right one. Edit this file for macOS; the
#   Linux variant is its sibling. Everything except the theme block is
#   identical — keep it that way.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   bat's configuration, read from ~/.config/bat/config. The format is unusual:
#   each line is a COMMAND-LINE ARGUMENT exactly as you would type it, not a
#   key/value pair. Lines starting with `#` are comments. Anything you pass on
#   the command line overrides what is set here.
#
#   QUOTING TRAP: a value containing spaces MUST be quoted, because each line
#   is split into arguments the way a shell would.
#   `--theme-dark=Catppuccin Mocha` becomes two arguments and bat then treats
#   "Mocha" as a filename to display. Write `--theme-dark="Catppuccin Mocha"`.
#
# WHERE ELSE bat IS USED
#   fish/conf.d/10-environment.fish routes $MANPAGER through bat, so these
#   settings shape man pages too. The Neovim config does not use bat.
#
#   Full option list: `bat --help`. Themes: `bat --list-themes`.
# ==============================================================================

# --- Theme --------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: `auto:system` reads the macOS light/dark preference and picks between
#       --theme-light and --theme-dark accordingly.
# WHY : Matches the rest of the stack, which all follows the system appearance
#       (Ghostty, Zed, Neovim). The previous setting was `ansi`, which renders
#       using only the terminal's 16 palette colours — that guarantees an exact
#       match with Ghostty's theme and flips automatically, but it is visually
#       flat because it cannot use more than 16 colours. Catppuccin is a
#       matched light/dark pair designed for exactly this kind of switching.
# HOW : Revert to the terminal palette with a single line: --theme="ansi"
#       Other bundled options: "auto" (detect from terminal background),
#       "dark"/"light" (force one), or any name from --list-themes.
--theme=auto:system
--theme-dark="Catppuccin Mocha"
--theme-light="Catppuccin Latte"

# --- Decorations ----------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: Which parts of bat's chrome to draw.
# WHY : The `numbers,changes,header` trio is the useful subset: line numbers to refer
#       to, Git modification marks in the gutter, and a filename header so you
#       know what you are looking at. The default `full` adds a grid of box
#       lines around everything, which mostly wastes width.
# HOW : Other components: grid, rule, snip, header-filename, header-filesize.
#       `--style=plain` turns all of it off. Use -p on the command line for a
#       one-off plain view.
--style=numbers,changes,header

# --- Text rendering ---------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: Render italics in themes that use them (comments, mostly).
# WHY : Off by default because some terminals render italics as inverse video.
#       Ghostty handles them correctly, and JetBrains Mono has a real italic.
--italic-text=always

# WHAT: How wide a tab character displays.
# WHY : 4, matching tab_size in Zed and shiftwidth in Neovim.
--tabs=4

# --- Syntax mappings ---------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: Force a syntax for files bat cannot identify from their name.
# WHY : Several configs in this very repo have no extension and would otherwise
#       render as plain text. INI is the closest match for both formats.
# HOW : `--map-syntax='<glob>:<syntax name>'`. List syntax names with
#       `bat --list-languages`. The glob matches the whole path.
--map-syntax=*/ghostty/config:INI
--map-syntax=*/git/config:INI
--map-syntax=*/git/config.work:INI

# --- Documented extension points (inactive) --------------------------------------------
# WHAT: When to page output. `auto` (the default) pages only when the content
#       is taller than the terminal.
# --paging=auto
#
# WHAT: Line wrapping: auto, never, or character.
# --wrap=auto
#
# WHAT: Use a different pager. By default bat uses `less` with the right flags.
# --pager=less -FR
